<p>A banner is read in half a second, so it says one thing: who. On a cream ground with a faint grid (or near-black — <code>im-studio-dark</code>), the portrait fills the right and fades into the ground, the name sits in the band every device keeps, set huge, with the brand’s dot as its full stop. Below, the way YouTube shows it on a desk — cropped to that band, with the profile and the channel row under it — which is the only honest test of a banner.</p>
